    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sea-level fluctuations throughout the Pleistocene and Holocene episodically fragmented Caribbean island systems, resulting in unique evolutionary trajectories reflected in species-level phylogeographic patterns. I studied the morphologic differentiation between three lizard species in the genus Ameiva (Family Teiidae) and the biogeographic factors that explain speciation. Ameiva is an emblematic reptile of the Caribbean, and is also found throughout Central and South America. Two species thought to be sister taxa (A. plei and A. corax) are endemic to the Anguillan bank, a Caribbean island group in the northern Lesser Antilles while a separate species (A. corvina) is endemic to small, nearby Sombrero Island. I assessed diversity of these species of Ameiva by using 49 phenotypic traits, including scale characters, morphometric data, and melanism in A. corax and A. corvina. Using these data I constructed a phylogeny, which resulted in a more complete set of relationships than previously existed. My results and previous research of Ameiva suggest that melanistic coloration and other convergent morphological traits may have evolved independently in two of these lizard species in response similar environmental conditions.  My analysis is the first morphological phylogeny of these three lizards assessed in the context of island biogeography. Future studies should consider further testing these relationship with genetic data, island area change analyses, and assessment of speciation via reproductive isolation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
